$ hack-browser-data -h
hack-browser-data decrypts and exports browser data from Chromium-based
browsers, Firefox, and Safari on Windows, macOS, and Linux.

GitHub: https://github.com/moonD4rk/HackBrowserData

Usage:
  hack-browser-data [flags]
  hack-browser-data [command]

Available Commands:
  archive     Pack decryption-relevant profile files into a zip for cross-host restore
  dump        Extract and decrypt browser data (default command)
  dumpkeys    Export Chromium master keys as JSON for cross-host decryption
  help        Help about any command
  list        List detected browsers and profiles
  restore     Decrypt copied profile data using exported master keys
  version     Print version information

Flags:
  -b, --browser string                   target browser: all|<browsers> (default "all")
  -c, --category string                  data categories (comma-separated): all|password,cookie,bookmark,history,download,creditcard,extension,localstorage,sessionstorage (default "all")
  -d, --dir string                       output directory (default "results")
      --firefox-pw string                Firefox primary password (visible in ps and shell history)
      --firefox-pw-stdin[=true|false]    read the Firefox primary password from the first line of stdin
  -f, --format string                    output format: csv|json|cookie-editor (default "json")
  -h, --help[=true|false]                help for hack-browser-data
      --keychain-pw string               macOS keychain password (visible in ps and shell history)
      --keychain-pw-stdin[=true|false]   read the macOS keychain password from the first line of stdin
      --max-storage-value int            max storage value chars, 0=unlimited (default 2048)
  -p, --profile-path string              custom profile dir path, get with chrome://version
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]             enable debug logging
      --zip[=true|false]                 compress output to zip

Use "hack-browser-data [command] --help" for more information about a command.
$ hack-browser-data dump -h
Extract and decrypt browser data (default command)

Usage:
  hack-browser-data dump [flags]

Examples:
  hack-browser-data dump
  hack-browser-data dump -b chrome -c password,cookie
  hack-browser-data dump -b chrome -f json -d output
  hack-browser-data dump -f cookie-editor
  hack-browser-data dump --zip

Flags:
  -b, --browser string                   target browser: all|<browsers> (default "all")
  -c, --category string                  data categories (comma-separated): all|password,cookie,bookmark,history,download,creditcard,extension,localstorage,sessionstorage (default "all")
  -d, --dir string                       output directory (default "results")
      --firefox-pw string                Firefox primary password (visible in ps and shell history)
      --firefox-pw-stdin[=true|false]    read the Firefox primary password from the first line of stdin
  -f, --format string                    output format: csv|json|cookie-editor (default "json")
  -h, --help[=true|false]                help for dump
      --keychain-pw string               macOS keychain password (visible in ps and shell history)
      --keychain-pw-stdin[=true|false]   read the macOS keychain password from the first line of stdin
      --max-storage-value int            max storage value chars, 0=unlimited (default 2048)
  -p, --profile-path string              custom profile dir path, get with chrome://version
      --zip[=true|false]                 compress output to zip

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
$ hack-browser-data dumpkeys -h
Export Chromium master keys as JSON for cross-host decryption

Usage:
  hack-browser-data dumpkeys [flags]

Examples:
  hack-browser-data dumpkeys -o keys.json
  hack-browser-data dumpkeys -b chrome

Flags:
  -b, --browser string                   target browser: all|<browsers> (default "all")
  -h, --help[=true|false]                help for dumpkeys
      --keychain-pw string               macOS keychain password (visible in ps and shell history)
      --keychain-pw-stdin[=true|false]   read the macOS keychain password from the first line of stdin
  -o, --output string                    output file (default: stdout)

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
$ hack-browser-data archive -h
Pack decryption-relevant profile files into a zip for cross-host restore

Usage:
  hack-browser-data archive [flags]

Examples:
  hack-browser-data archive
  hack-browser-data archive -b chrome -c cookie -o chrome-cookies.zip

Flags:
  -b, --browser string      target browser: all|<browsers> (default "all")
  -c, --category string     data categories (comma-separated): all|password,cookie,bookmark,history,download,creditcard,extension,localstorage,sessionstorage (default "all")
  -h, --help[=true|false]   help for archive
  -o, --output string       output archive of decryption-relevant browser files (default "browser-data.zip")

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
$ hack-browser-data restore -h
Decrypt copied profile data using exported master keys

Usage:
  hack-browser-data restore [flags]

Examples:
  hack-browser-data restore --keys keys.json --data-zip data.zip
  hack-browser-data restore --keys keys.json --data-dir ./data -b chrome -c cookie
  hack-browser-data restore --keys keys.json --data-dir ./chrome-userdata -b chrome
  ssh origin "hack-browser-data dumpkeys" | hack-browser-data restore --keys - --data-zip data.zip

Flags:
  -b, --browser string          restore only this browser (optional; must match a vault in --keys)
  -c, --category string         data categories (comma-separated): all|password,cookie,bookmark,history,download,creditcard,extension,localstorage,sessionstorage (default "all")
      --data-dir string         copied profile data dir (archive layout, or one browser's User Data with -b)
      --data-zip string         zip produced by the archive command (alternative to --data-dir)
  -d, --dir string              output directory (default "results")
  -f, --format string           output format: csv|json|cookie-editor (default "json")
  -h, --help[=true|false]       help for restore
      --keys string             keys file from dumpkeys (use - for stdin)
      --max-storage-value int   max storage value chars, 0=unlimited (default 2048)
      --zip[=true|false]        compress output to zip

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
$ hack-browser-data list -h
List detected browsers and profiles

Usage:
  hack-browser-data list [flags]

Examples:
  hack-browser-data list
  hack-browser-data list --detail

Flags:
      --detail[=true|false]   show per-category entry counts
  -h, --help[=true|false]     help for list

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
$ hack-browser-data version -h
Print version information

Usage:
  hack-browser-data version [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help[=true|false]   help for version

Global Flags:
  -v, --verbose[=true|false]   enable debug logging
